## Zed (Claude's reading, 2026-11-22 and 2026-11-27)

Zed is Luke Hutchison's language (github.com/zedlanguage/zed). Read at commit a02fbb3 (2026-11-27);
the passages below are unchanged from d6d0669 (2026-11-22). Avik's interest in it (2026-11-22): "guy
behind it has fastest SAT solver in the world unreleased so I'm very interested in his work". No
public source for the solver was found on 2026-11-22, and the Zed repository contains none.

### What Zed is, from its README

- Hutchison's design began to take shape around 2006. The README says the detailed spec became "a
  perfect prompt" once Opus 5.5 existed, and that Zed is "being fully built out in 2026 from this
  complex design doc developed over the last 20 years" (README line 2724).
- It is a dataflow language. Each value is defined once and the compiler orders the work from the
  dependency graph. Output is bit-identical at any core count, floating-point reductions included,
  with an enumerated list of exceptions: arrival order at ports, a declared output interleaving,
  foreign-call order the program leaves open, `unordered` reductions, running out of a resource
  (line 2501).
- Incremental computation needs no annotation. A changed input passes through the same graph as a
  "wave", only what it affects is recomputed, and the result equals a full recomputation (line 2119).
- Build time and run time use one evaluator. `Json.decode` given a literal runs during the build;
  given a run-time value it is specialized to the expected type (line 2243).
- A `law` gets a grade: exhaustively checked, proved, checked up to a bound, tested, assumed or
  unknown. A law that is only tested stops the build, but an `@associative` annotation that is only
  tested still compiles, with one fixed regrouping (line 2404).
- A `grammar` gives a parser and a serializer from one definition, and the compiler checks that the
  round trip gives back the value (line 2291).
- Facets (units, taint, or one a program defines) are facts tracked through every operation at
  compile time.
- Ranges and refinements are decided by linear arithmetic, with "no SMT solver", by a fixed set of
  procedures that always finish (line 2153). The deciders are in `crates/zedc-solvers`: two-variable
  and three-variable deciders (Lenstra's integer programming in dimension three), Megiddo's
  fixed-dimension linear programming, LLL reduction, a negative-cycle search and the UTVPI closure.

### The SAT boundary: `crates/zed-kernel/src/types/empty.rs`

- Subtyping is emptiness: `a <: b` iff `a & !b` is empty (file comment, lines 1–2).
- The tuple part of a type at each length is a Boolean combination of tuple types. It is decided on
  a box, one type per component, starting from the whole product: each tuple type holds the box,
  misses it, or is open on it; narrowing shrinks the box, and splitting divides it (lines 3–24).
- The procedure is sound and polynomial and is not complete, because emptiness of Boolean
  combinations of tuple types is coNP-hard (lines 26–28). A cover of the box by tuple types is a
  DNF tautology (line 7616).
- What it gives up: a part that only a split of the box in two or more components at once proves
  empty counts as inhabited. `Type::verdict` answers `Refused(TupleSplit)` unless a value is found
  first; the subtyping check then fails, and the error names the types and the cause so that the
  program states the case split itself (lines 28–32).
- The file names what completes it: "A complete solution would split the box in every component (a
  DPLL search over the tuple types, exponential in them), or hand the part to a SAT solver." (lines
  43–44)
- One case is decided completely. When every open tuple type is open on one component alone, the
  part is a 2-CNF, which `TwoSat::solve` decides in time linear in the part; the reading into
  clauses is Plaisted and Greenbaum's (lines 7789–7801).
- 3-SAT is in the checker explicitly. Random 3-CNF formulas encoded as types are refused in tests
  (`random_3_cnf_found_by_a_second_pass_or_an_undone_cut_or_refused`,
  `the_verdict_of_random_3_cnf_is_refused_in_its_size`). The comment at lines 12505–12507 says that
  no polynomial rule finds a value wherever there is one, since that would decide 3-SAT. Covering
  list lengths by residue classes is NP-hard by a reduction from 3-SAT, checked in
  `the_three_sat_reduction_at_residue_length_is_correct` (lines 3215–3223).

Zed's checker refuses exactly where a complete SAT procedure would decide; a SAT solver at that call
site would turn each `Refused(TupleSplit)` into empty or a value.

### What is true about Zed and hyperactivity

**Zed's guarantees are one fact stated many times.** Every headline property of Zed is an equality
between two representations of one computation:

| Zed's guarantee | the two representations identified |
|---|---|
| incremental result equals full recomputation | the result maintained by waves, the result recomputed |
| bit-identical at any core count | a parallel schedule, the sequential one |
| one evaluator at build and run time | the value computed in the build, the value computed at run time |
| `Json.decode` specialized to `User` | the specialized decoder, the generic one |
| grammar round trip | parse after serialize, the identity |
| `@associative` regrouping | one grouping of a reduction, another |

Zed obtains each by a separate construction in its compiler, and each holds only inside that
construction. In hyperactivity these are one operation: an identification of representations is a
path, and transport along it carries every construction built over one representation to the other,
operations and laws together (Part I §4, §4.1, (4.12)). Zed has six special cases of univalence,
built by hand and not composable with each other or with anything a program defines. A seventh
property in Zed is a seventh compiler feature; in hyperactivity it is a program.

**The type checker refuses where the problem is higher-dimensional.** Zed decides `a <: b` as
emptiness of `a & !b`. The box is a product of component types, a cube; each tuple type is a product
of per-component types, a subcube; the question is whether a union of subcubes covers the cube (a DNF
tautology, line 7616), whose complement is CNF satisfiability. Zed's procedure is complete when the
cover can be settled by cutting along one component at a time, and the 2-CNF case it decides in
linear time is exactly the case where each open subcube is open along one axis. It refuses
(`Refused(TupleSplit)`) exactly when only a cut in two or more components at once settles it: when
the cover has cells that meet in genuinely higher dimension. What Zed sees of a cover is a bit: covers
or not. How the cells meet, which faces they share and how a cut on one face continues across
another, is the cubical structure of the cover, and Zed discards it. A hyperactive representation of
the same question keeps the cover as a cubical cell complex, with its faces and their identifications
as data; the information that the one-axis procedure lacks is the higher face structure that a cell
complex carries by construction. This is the setting in which satisfiability is the emptiness of the
complement of a union of subcubes, and the place where path content over the cells gives algorithms
that a bitwise view of the cover does not have.

**Refusal is a proof the human is made to write and the system then forgets.** On `TupleSplit`
Zed's error names the types and the cause, "so the program states the case split itself" (lines
30–32). The programmer writes the split, Zed accepts it, and the split remains source text: it is not
a value any other check can use. In hyperactivity a case split is a construction, and the routine,
its relation and its rationale are one term (Part I §1): written once, it is retained and reusable
wherever its premises hold. Zed's design keeps compile time predictable by making the human supply
the reasoning; hyperactivity keeps the reasoning once it exists.

**Checking as a separate ladder versus normalization.** Zed's laws climb a fixed ladder of methods
and get a grade, and a law that is only tested can still license a regrouping. In hyperactivity
there is no separate judging layer: a declaration normalizes to an inhabitant or to empty, and Part
I §4.4 shows the form (`msort`: the declaration computes, and its answer type is contractible). A
grade of "tested" has no counterpart because nothing is admitted that is not constructed.

**Zed already contains dependency-certified reuse, inside one checker.** Its emptiness check is
coinductive: a type met again is assumed empty, results are kept, and "a result ... that rests on an
assumption later found false is dropped, and only such a result" (file comment). That is a
dependency certificate (Part I §10.5) built privately for one decision procedure. Clause learning
in a SAT solver is the same thing again: a proof that a sub-box is empty, kept and reused on every
other branch it applies to, which is reuse of a reduction under substitution (Part I §6.1, (6.8)–
(6.9)). In hyperactivity this reuse is the general rule of every computation, not a mechanism
rebuilt inside each solver.

**What this means for Hutchison's solver.** A SAT solver is a fast construction of Dec(Sat f) (Part
I Appendix A.1), whose trivial construction is enumeration. In Zed it would enter as one more
special procedure behind `verdict`. In hyperactivity it is a program whose results, learned clauses
included, are retained constructions available to every other program, and whose box covers keep
their cell structure.

